{"name":"Glossy Gold","hex":"#ffdd77","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"glossy","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a smooth, silklike, reflective (shiny) surface.","Attention-grabbing and superficially attractive."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A glossy magazine.","A glossy photograph."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/glossy"},{"term":"gold","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A heavy yellow elemental metal of great value, with atomic number 79.","A coin or coinage made of this material, or supposedly so."]},{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["☉ (alchemy)"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/gold"}],"entries":[{"title":"Gloss (optics)","blocks":[{"text":"Gloss is an optical property which indicates how well a surface reflects light in a specular (mirror-like) direction. It is one of the important parameters that are used to describe the visual appearance of an object. Other categories of visual appearance related to the perception of regular or diffuse reflection and transmission of light have been organized under the concept of cesia in an order system with three variables, including gloss among the involved aspects. The factors that affect gloss are the refractive index of the material, the angle of incident light and the surface texture."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5d/Gloss_light_Illuminates.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gloss_(optics)","wikidata":"Q900581"},{"title":"Gold","blocks":[{"text":"Gold is a chemical element; its chemical symbol is Au and atomic number 79. In its pure form, it is a bright-metallic-yellow, dense, soft, malleable, and ductile metal. Chemically, gold is a transition metal, a group 11 element, and one of the noble metals. It is one of the least reactive chemical elements, being the second lowest in the reactivity series, with only platinum ranked as less reactive. Gold is solid under standard conditions."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d7/Gold-crystals.jpg/330px-Gold-crystals.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold","wikidata":"Q897"},{"title":"Gold (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Gold, also called golden, is a color tone resembling the chemical element gold."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5b/GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg/330px-GoldNuggetUSGOV.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_(color)","wikidata":"Q208045"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Nanorough.gif/330px-Nanorough.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Nanorough.gif/330px-Nanorough.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Figure1: Specular reflection of light from a rough surface","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANanorough.gif"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/59/Specular_gloss.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/59/Specular_gloss.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASpecular_gloss.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/da/Sheen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/da/Sheen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASheen.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/co